# NIP-AB device-pairing sidecar (Cofoundy / Railway).
#
# WHY THIS EXISTS
# ---------------
# Scanning the pairing QR in Buzz Mobile fails with
#   `WebSocket connection failed: HTTP error: 404 Not Found`
# when the relay does not advertise a pairing relay. Without
# `BUZZ_PAIRING_RELAY_URL` set, the main relay omits `pairing_relay_url` from
# its NIP-11 document and clients fall back to the legacy same-host `/pair`
# convention — a path the relay has never served. Upstream names the failure
# in crates/buzz-relay/src/nip11.rs: "open relays misroutes pairing peers to a
# non-existent /pair sidecar".
#
# The fix is this service plus `BUZZ_PAIRING_RELAY_URL` on the main relay.
#
# WHAT IT IS
# ----------
# `buzz-pair-relay` is an ephemeral, STATELESS relay that brokers NIP-AB
# handshakes between a desktop and a phone. It holds no database, needs no
# volume, and keeps pairing state only in memory for the life of a handshake.
# Restarting it drops in-flight pairings and nothing else.
#
# The binary already ships inside the upstream image at
# /usr/local/bin/buzz-pair-relay — this wrapper only starts it. Keep BUZZ_IMAGE
# byte-identical to deploy/railway/Dockerfile so both services run the same
# build; a drift between them is a version skew nobody would think to check.
#
# NOTE ON EXPOSURE: this endpoint is intentionally public and unauthenticated —
# that is the NIP-AB design (upstream runs wss://pairing.buzz.xyz the same way).
# Security comes from the handshake itself, not from network reachability.
ARG BUZZ_IMAGE=ghcr.io/block/buzz:sha-b1b283c
FROM ${BUZZ_IMAGE}

# No volume to chown, so unlike the main relay's wrapper this never needs root.
USER buzz

COPY deploy/railway/pair-relay-entrypoint.sh /usr/local/bin/buzz-pair-relay-entrypoint.sh

EXPOSE 5000

ENTRYPOINT ["/usr/local/bin/buzz-pair-relay-entrypoint.sh"]
